00:01:53.505 --> 00:01:56.131
Sherose: Welcome to WordPress
Accessibility Day 2026.

00:01:56.163 --> 00:01:58.943
My name is Sherose Badruddin, and I'm
a digital accessibility consultant

00:01:58.944 --> 00:02:00.550
at the University of North Carolina.

00:02:01.024 --> 00:02:03.946
Thank you for joining us for
Accessibility is Not Enough: What We

00:02:03.947 --> 00:02:07.223
Learned Building Websites for The DOT
Experience, presented by Carl Michel,

00:02:07.499 --> 00:02:09.332
Melody Partain, and Danielle Burton.

00:02:09.955 --> 00:02:12.750
Carl is the creative technology
director at Fieldtrip, where he shapes

00:02:12.751 --> 00:02:15.481
the tech strategy and development
behind the agency's digital work.

00:02:15.885 --> 00:02:19.547
His expertise spans website architecture,
content management systems, accessible

00:02:19.548 --> 00:02:22.835
development, third-party integrations,
and long-term platform planning.

00:02:23.188 --> 00:02:26.149
Carl works closely with strategists
and designers to turn ambitious

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:29.965
ideas into technically sound digital
experiences that organizations

00:02:29.965 --> 00:02:31.708
can confidently manage and evolve.

00:02:32.432 --> 00:02:35.703
Melody brings structure, strategy,
and a user-first perspective to

00:02:35.708 --> 00:02:37.013
Fieldtrip's website projects.

00:02:37.408 --> 00:02:41.040
As director of strategy and integration,
she leads project management and user

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.959
experience strategy, helping organizations
translate complex goals and audience

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:48.787
needs into digital experiences that
are clear, intuitive, and accessible.

00:02:50.084 --> 00:02:52.930
Danielle Burton works at the American
Printing House for the Blind as the

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:54.781
communications accessibility editor.

00:02:55.233 --> 00:02:58.162
She has been working in the field of
digital accessibility for four years.

00:02:58.562 --> 00:03:01.021
She is also a deaf-blind
individual who relies on assistive

00:03:01.021 --> 00:03:02.655
technology for access every day.

00:03:03.559 --> 00:03:07.026
Please feel free to add your questions
in the Zoom Q&A section, and we'll

00:03:07.026 --> 00:03:08.462
answer them at the end of the session.

00:03:08.838 --> 00:03:10.876
Use the chat to connect
with other attendees.

00:03:14.716 --> 00:03:16.702
Melody: Hello, and thank
you for joining us today.

00:03:17.296 --> 00:03:20.009
Most of us working on websites
want to believe we're building

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:21.799
experiences that work for everyone.

00:03:22.242 --> 00:03:24.725
We do our best to follow
accessibility guidelines.

00:03:24.786 --> 00:03:27.281
We try to decipher a
checklist of requirements.

00:03:27.626 --> 00:03:30.265
But through one project, we
learned that a website can check

00:03:30.266 --> 00:03:32.030
the boxes and still fall short.

00:03:32.492 --> 00:03:34.896
Today, we want to share the
project that changed the way

00:03:34.934 --> 00:03:36.539
we think about accessibility.

00:03:38.294 --> 00:03:42.058
Most clients see building an accessible
website as a check-the-box requirement.

00:03:42.337 --> 00:03:43.058
So did we.

00:03:44.848 --> 00:03:46.627
But what if we changed our target?

00:03:47.433 --> 00:03:50.541
Maybe accessibility isn't
actually the finish line.

00:03:52.799 --> 00:03:57.543
What if instead we took a step further and
shot for inclusive digital experiences?

00:03:58.032 --> 00:03:59.066
What's the difference?

00:03:59.519 --> 00:04:03.240
Accessible means someone can
use your website, but inclusive

00:04:03.288 --> 00:04:06.027
means they feel like the website
was built with them in mind.

00:04:06.644 --> 00:04:08.995
They are not experiencing
a diminished product.

00:04:12.093 --> 00:04:16.165
To explore, let's start by going back
a bit and introducing the project and

00:04:16.166 --> 00:04:18.259
the team that changed our perspective.

00:04:20.668 --> 00:04:23.355
American Printing House for
the Blind is pioneering The Dot

00:04:23.355 --> 00:04:25.300
Experience, opening later this month.

00:04:25.851 --> 00:04:28.682
The Dot Experience is the most
inclusive museum in the world.

00:04:29.228 --> 00:04:34.162
They have made every exhibit, image, and
artifact accessible to everyone, including

00:04:34.164 --> 00:04:36.089
people who may not be able to see them.

00:04:37.863 --> 00:04:41.686
While this  museum experience is new to
American Printing House for the Blind

00:04:41.688 --> 00:04:46.243
roster, they have a deep history of
creating accessible learning experiences

00:04:46.258 --> 00:04:50.850
through educational, workplace, and
independent living products and services

00:04:50.854 --> 00:04:52.638
for people who are blind and low vision.

00:04:53.576 --> 00:04:57.111
This includes everything from
Braille literacy tools and accessible

00:04:57.111 --> 00:05:01.140
textbooks to tactile graphics,
assistive technology, and more.

00:05:03.962 --> 00:05:07.288
Today you'll be hearing from myself
and Carl Michel from Fieldtrip, an

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:11.801
agency from Louisville, Kentucky, that
specializes in amplifying the impact of

00:05:11.804 --> 00:05:15.736
cause-driven organizations, and Danielle
Burton from American Printing House for

00:05:15.738 --> 00:05:20.127
the Blind, an organization that is br-
breaking down barriers for people who

00:05:20.127 --> 00:05:22.628
are blind and low vision since 1858.

00:05:23.513 --> 00:05:24.524
My name is Melody.

00:05:24.659 --> 00:05:27.193
I am Director of Strategy and
Integration here at Fieldtrip

00:05:27.245 --> 00:05:28.802
and lead for this project.

00:05:29.404 --> 00:05:33.518
My role was to understand the needs of
both our users on the website, inclusive

00:05:33.549 --> 00:05:37.962
of different abilities, and understanding
the business goals that The Dot Experience

00:05:37.962 --> 00:05:40.110
itself needed the websites to fulfill.

00:05:40.733 --> 00:05:43.884
Ultimately, we had to create a
strategy that supported both.

00:05:46.238 --> 00:05:46.938
Carl: Hello everyone.

00:05:47.032 --> 00:05:48.053
I'm Carl Michel.

00:05:48.275 --> 00:05:52.051
I'm the creative technology director
here at Fieldtrip, where I help

00:05:52.079 --> 00:05:53.974
lead the web development work.

00:05:54.928 --> 00:05:59.174
For The Dot Experience, I was responsible
for the development of the website,

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:04.265
working closely with the team to take
what we were learning about accessibility

00:06:04.707 --> 00:06:08.959
and translate it into how the site
was actually built and experienced.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:12.432
Danielle: Hi, everyone.

00:06:12.491 --> 00:06:17.343
My name is Danielle Burton, and
I work at the American Printing

00:06:17.351 --> 00:06:22.243
House for the Blind as the
communications accessibility editor.

00:06:22.647 --> 00:06:28.585
I am also a prototyper for
The Dot Experience museum.

00:06:29.319 --> 00:06:34.774
My main role for this particular
project was to ensure that digital

00:06:34.774 --> 00:06:40.469
accessibility was included from the
very beginning of the website and

00:06:40.481 --> 00:06:42.479
all of The Dot Experience itself.

00:06:43.409 --> 00:06:50.172
And I also brought to the table as a user
of, of assistive technology myself, being

00:06:50.172 --> 00:06:56.675
deaf-blind, had a unique insight into
building the accessibility in the website.

00:06:58.785 --> 00:07:01.209
Melody: While the three of us are
here to speak with you today, this

00:07:01.221 --> 00:07:03.257
project had a much deeper roster.

00:07:03.601 --> 00:07:06.497
Our team brought together people
with different specialties, lived

00:07:06.497 --> 00:07:09.041
experiences, needs, and perspectives.

00:07:09.378 --> 00:07:12.629
We also brought in experts in
accessibility and inclusive website

00:07:12.631 --> 00:07:16.251
design and development to challenge
our thinking and help guide our work.

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:18.912
That range of perspectives
was intentional.

00:07:19.137 --> 00:07:23.190
If we were going to create an experience
designed to include more people, we needed

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.245
more people in the process of creating it.

00:07:29.055 --> 00:07:33.590
Danielle: One of the things we
did is we looked at different

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:37.316
We included different accessibility
features in The Dot Experience.

00:07:37.575 --> 00:07:42.807
An example of this is
the Helen Keller Gallery.

00:07:43.267 --> 00:07:50.190
And in this gallery, we included
various pieces of accessibility.

00:07:50.732 --> 00:08:00.178
Some of these included captions on the
video, interactive videos, ASL or American

00:08:00.178 --> 00:08:06.672
Sign Language interpretation on the
videos, and we also included a transcript

00:08:06.868 --> 00:08:12.957
with a refreshable Braille device
at the station for users to read the

00:08:12.958 --> 00:08:14.832
transcript for those that are deaf/blind.

00:08:16.219 --> 00:08:23.346
In addition to creating the digital
accessibility, we did create the physical

00:08:23.346 --> 00:08:32.656
accessibility of navigating the gallery
with buttons carefully designed for a

00:08:32.656 --> 00:08:35.263
wide range of users of all disabilities.

00:08:35.299 --> 00:08:41.537
The buttons were tested with a group of
individuals that had various disabilities

00:08:41.549 --> 00:08:47.949
for layout, clarity, easy to use, and
anything else that we could think of.

00:08:48.967 --> 00:08:56.838
In addition to the media in the gallery,
there will also be 3D representations of

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:59.971
various objects that are behind glass.

00:09:00.798 --> 00:09:05.264
There is also a 3D model of Helen
Keller herself sitting at her

00:09:05.264 --> 00:09:07.196
desk that can be explored by touch

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:15.792
Another aspect of the accessibility
of this museum is the floor.

00:09:15.795 --> 00:09:21.972
And we also used specific textures on
the floor to mean that this is gallery

00:09:21.972 --> 00:09:27.120
space versus this is a path of travel
and to, from gallery to gallery.

00:09:27.282 --> 00:09:32.860
So all of this combined we- creates
an accessible museum experience.

00:09:35.289 --> 00:09:37.445
Melody: The examples
paint a clear picture.

00:09:37.798 --> 00:09:41.152
Countless hours of research,
innovation, and user testing

00:09:41.166 --> 00:09:44.682
have been poured into making this
groundbreaking experience a reality.

00:09:45.092 --> 00:09:47.657
The website needed to live
up to that same standard.

00:09:48.373 --> 00:09:51.247
It couldn't simply be a website
about an inclusive museum.

00:09:51.288 --> 00:09:55.423
The digital experience itself needed to
embody that same commitment to inclusion.

00:09:57.174 --> 00:09:59.734
So how does Fieldtrip
fit into this project?

00:10:00.187 --> 00:10:02.943
You'll notice that when I
introduced Fieldtrip, accessibility

00:10:02.944 --> 00:10:04.569
was not listed as our niche.

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:09.158
We did not win this project because of a
long list of highly accessible websites.

00:10:09.722 --> 00:10:13.135
In fact, there was not one website
in our case studies that hit the

00:10:13.135 --> 00:10:16.493
mark of accessibility that we
were targeting for this project.

00:10:17.771 --> 00:10:20.979
What we did have was the willingness
to admit what we did not know

00:10:21.295 --> 00:10:25.068
and an eagerness to learn, to do
better for this website and for the

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:26.634
websites we touch in the future.

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:31.737
Audience-centered strategy has always
been core to how we approach website work.

00:10:32.161 --> 00:10:36.192
That means stepping away from the wish
list of the loudest voices in the room

00:10:36.208 --> 00:10:40.092
and instead focusing in on the people
who benefit from the work the most.

00:10:40.817 --> 00:10:44.725
For this project, that same process became
our starting point, but we were going

00:10:44.725 --> 00:10:48.494
to have to expand our understanding of
what truly designing around a user meant.

00:10:51.009 --> 00:10:55.992
Carl: We have worked with organizations
solving homelessness in Portland,

00:10:55.992 --> 00:11:00.759
Oregon, providing healthcare for those
who need it most here in Louisville,

00:11:01.422 --> 00:11:06.688
and fighting for equality in women's
health research across the country.

00:11:08.090 --> 00:11:12.612
In all these projects, our
focus was on the primary user,

00:11:13.499 --> 00:11:15.338
those who need the site most.

00:11:16.275 --> 00:11:21.834
By leading the project with that group top
of mind, we created stronger experiences

00:11:21.853 --> 00:11:24.117
to support the organization as a whole.

00:11:25.034 --> 00:11:29.956
Th-this approach was still highly
relevant to The Dot Experience project

00:11:30.421 --> 00:11:34.642
and set us up to follow our standard
approach to better understand this

00:11:34.673 --> 00:11:37.468
community and their specific needs online.

00:11:39.313 --> 00:11:41.490
We also have a deep
knowledge of WordPress.

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:46.336
I myself have been developing in the
WordPress environment for about 15 years.

00:11:47.073 --> 00:11:51.781
We pride ourselves in fully l-
leaning into the ecosystem to create

00:11:51.812 --> 00:11:57.188
websites that are easy to manage,
meet client goals, and often live

00:11:57.200 --> 00:11:59.597
past the standard website lifespan.

00:12:01.098 --> 00:12:06.658
So we came into this project with
a process we trusted, a platform we

00:12:06.658 --> 00:12:12.788
deeply knew, and we also came in with
some assumptions about accessibility

00:12:12.941 --> 00:12:14.368
that were about to be challenged.

00:12:17.473 --> 00:12:19.840
Melody: So let's talk
about those assumptions.

00:12:20.495 --> 00:12:24.820
The first was you have to choose
between accessibility and great design.

00:12:25.765 --> 00:12:29.400
That to have something that is highly
accessible inherently means that you're

00:12:29.401 --> 00:12:32.114
sacrificing visual interest or immersion.

00:12:33.238 --> 00:12:38.478
Number two, an accessibility plugin alone
can fill in the gaps for your website

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:40.464
to create an accessible experience.

00:12:41.621 --> 00:12:45.007
If a website wasn't accessible at
its foundation, there are tools

00:12:45.008 --> 00:12:47.752
like plugins that we can layer
on top to help get you there.

00:12:49.122 --> 00:12:53.943
And lastly, if you're not using a plugin,
a more custom approach to accessibility

00:12:53.959 --> 00:12:57.906
is too expensive or time-consuming
for a typical website project.

00:12:58.578 --> 00:13:02.688
The work that goes into it would increase
client budgets and timelines s- to the

00:13:02.688 --> 00:13:04.449
point that they were no longer doable.

00:13:06.657 --> 00:13:08.207
That brings us to discovery.

00:13:08.978 --> 00:13:12.736
Before we could start designing solutions,
we needed to challenge those assumptions

00:13:12.737 --> 00:13:16.207
I just listed and more importantly,
understand what we didn't know.

00:13:16.866 --> 00:13:21.016
We couldn't rely solely on accessibility
guidelines or a checklist of requirements.

00:13:21.397 --> 00:13:23.995
We needed to understand how
people with different abilities

00:13:24.056 --> 00:13:25.655
actually experience the web.

00:13:27.257 --> 00:13:30.484
Our first order of business was sitting
with users who utilize assistive

00:13:30.486 --> 00:13:35.487
technologies to experience firsthand how
inequitable the online experience can be.

00:13:36.127 --> 00:13:40.014
We wanted to learn this through the lens
of someone proficient in utilizing these

00:13:40.015 --> 00:13:44.989
technologies so that our lack of skill did
not hide some of the true frustrations.

00:13:48.126 --> 00:13:53.738
Danielle: The first step is we sat
down with Fieldtrip, uh, my… I

00:13:53.742 --> 00:13:59.610
myself sat down with Fieldtrip, and
we went through various demonstrations

00:13:59.688 --> 00:14:01.595
of utilizing a screen reader.

00:14:02.005 --> 00:14:09.071
I first demonstrated the ability to
navigate a website that was fairly usable

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:16.806
and accessible, and pointed out why the
website was accessible using a screen

00:14:16.806 --> 00:14:23.623
reader, such as heading navigation,
clearly labeled links, buttons, tab order.

00:14:24.872 --> 00:14:33.319
I also then demonstrated a website that
utilized poor accessibility design and

00:14:33.382 --> 00:14:40.201
pointed out why a poorly designed website
is difficult for a screen reader user

00:14:40.201 --> 00:14:47.731
to use, including illogical heading
structure, unlabeled buttons, keyboard

00:14:47.735 --> 00:14:53.654
traps, and anything else that we could
discover in exploring various websites.

00:14:59.198 --> 00:15:06.654
We also discussed how a screen reader
user might navigate a website that they

00:15:06.654 --> 00:15:11.503
are familiar with versus one that they
are not familiar with, and understanding

00:15:12.146 --> 00:15:17.250
those strategies so that the design
of The Dot Experience website could

00:15:18.132 --> 00:15:23.523
fully allow a user to utilize those
strategies effectively when they come

00:15:23.525 --> 00:15:27.087
and visit the website for themselves
and explore it for the first time.

00:15:30.714 --> 00:15:39.467
So one major example that was shown during
this first step was simply a shopping site

00:15:39.489 --> 00:15:43.119
where you add the, add a item to the cart.

00:15:43.538 --> 00:15:50.024
If the cart, add to cart button is not
accessible, then a user of assisted

00:15:50.025 --> 00:15:56.115
technology cannot independently make a
purchase, and this very simple everyday

00:15:56.115 --> 00:16:04.951
activity that we do every day can either
be easy or impossible for individuals with

00:16:04.965 --> 00:16:07.218
disabilities, depending on the design.

00:16:10.276 --> 00:16:13.882
Melody: Watching Danielle navigate
websites we use every day challenged the

00:16:13.883 --> 00:16:16.540
way we understood accessibility barriers.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:21.375
These were not obscure cases buried
deep within a website, and they

00:16:21.376 --> 00:16:23.466
also weren't small, unknown sites.

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:27.318
They were on sites we all visit
and were everyday interactions:

00:16:27.506 --> 00:16:33.064
navigation, headings, buttons, forms,
things that most of us move through

00:16:33.076 --> 00:16:34.648
without ever even considering.

00:16:35.739 --> 00:16:38.786
And when something wasn't built
correctly, Danielle was the one

00:16:38.825 --> 00:16:40.450
being asked to do the extra work.

00:16:40.875 --> 00:16:45.513
She had to figure out what was the website
meant, find another way through, or

00:16:45.537 --> 00:16:50.601
sometimes abandon the task altogether, and
that helped shift, shift our perspective.

00:16:51.615 --> 00:16:55.241
We stopped looking at accessibility
issues as technical errors that

00:16:55.242 --> 00:16:58.976
we needed to fix and instead
started seeing them as barriers we

00:16:58.976 --> 00:17:00.923
were responsible for preventing.

00:17:01.811 --> 00:17:04.825
It also challenged one of the
assumptions we brought into this

00:17:04.829 --> 00:17:08.587
project, that an accessibility
plugin could help fill the gaps.

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:12.739
As Danielle showed us how these
tools actually behave with a

00:17:12.739 --> 00:17:15.894
screen reader, we saw that adding
another layer of technology on

00:17:15.896 --> 00:17:19.644
top of an inaccessible foundation
doesn't really solve the problem.

00:17:20.089 --> 00:17:24.380
When implemented poorly, it can actually
create additional confusion and barriers.

00:17:25.467 --> 00:17:28.053
That became a pretty clear
line for this project.

00:17:28.247 --> 00:17:30.658
We weren't going to rely on
a plugin to make the website

00:17:30.658 --> 00:17:32.138
accessible after the fact.

00:17:32.502 --> 00:17:35.843
Accessibility had to be considered
in the structure, design, and

00:17:35.843 --> 00:17:37.588
development of the website itself.

00:17:40.728 --> 00:17:43.071
Carl: Now on to step two of discovery.

00:17:43.636 --> 00:17:45.099
We went back to the basics.

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:51.513
To build a truly inclusive website, we
first had to broaden our understanding

00:17:51.608 --> 00:17:54.006
of what accessibility actually means.

00:17:55.273 --> 00:17:58.741
Up until this point, much of our
focus had been centered around

00:17:58.744 --> 00:18:02.459
blindness because that was the primary
audience we were designing for.

00:18:03.372 --> 00:18:07.250
But accessibility is not about
accommodating a single disability,

00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:10.548
in the case of The Dot experience,
blindness and low vision.

00:18:11.535 --> 00:18:15.379
It is about designing for the
many different ways people

00:18:15.408 --> 00:18:19.472
perceive, understand, navigate,
and interact with the web.

00:18:20.806 --> 00:18:25.227
The POUR principles became our
guiding framework: perceivable,

00:18:25.616 --> 00:18:28.756
operable, understandable, and robust.

00:18:30.149 --> 00:18:35.713
Using these principles, we took a critical
look at websites we had built in the past,

00:18:36.227 --> 00:18:40.740
identifying where we had fallen short and
where our pros- process could improve.

00:18:41.839 --> 00:18:46.018
Those lessons became a foundation for
The Dot Experience, helping us move

00:18:46.022 --> 00:18:50.336
forward with a much more complete and
intentional approach to accessibility.

00:18:55.518 --> 00:19:00.202
Our final step of discovery was utilizing
assistive technologies ourselves.

00:19:00.917 --> 00:19:04.659
Watching Danielle use a screen reader
gave us insight into the world,

00:19:05.020 --> 00:19:09.350
but using them in, on our own took
that understanding a step further.

00:19:10.245 --> 00:19:16.456
We navigated websites, again, including
ones we had built, with screen readers and

00:19:16.517 --> 00:19:21.839
other tools to experience firsthand the
hurdles we had observed through Danielle

00:19:21.893 --> 00:19:23.584
and learned about in our research.

00:19:24.950 --> 00:19:31.229
That was valuable because accessibility
problems can often feel abstract when

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:34.182
they appear in guidelines or audits.

00:19:35.366 --> 00:19:40.022
When you are the person navigating
without a mouse, facing unclear

00:19:40.022 --> 00:19:45.213
links, poor heading structure,
or badly labeled buttons, those

00:19:45.229 --> 00:19:47.607
barriers become much more tangible.

00:19:49.118 --> 00:19:53.172
It also reinforced that
accessibility is about more than

00:19:53.172 --> 00:19:55.121
just being able to complete a task.

00:19:55.831 --> 00:19:58.225
The quality of that
experience matters too.

00:19:58.900 --> 00:20:03.945
If a website is technically usable
but requires extra effort, creates

00:20:03.955 --> 00:20:09.090
confusion, or force-forces workarounds,
it is still a bad experience.

00:20:10.214 --> 00:20:15.572
Using these technologies ourselves gave us
greater empathy, but more importantly, it

00:20:15.572 --> 00:20:17.601
made us better designers and developers.

00:20:18.184 --> 00:20:22.243
We began recognizing barriers
earlier, questioning assumptions,

00:20:22.359 --> 00:20:26.408
and considering accessibility through
the design and development process

00:20:26.648 --> 00:20:28.819
rather than testing for it at the end.

00:20:32.951 --> 00:20:36.394
Melody: With our research complete, we
moved on to the design of the website.

00:20:37.728 --> 00:20:40.768
The Dot Experience is pushing the
boundaries of what an in-person

00:20:40.768 --> 00:20:44.676
museum experience can be for people
with a wide variety of needs.

00:20:45.172 --> 00:20:48.192
We needed to bring that same
ambition to our website.

00:20:49.587 --> 00:20:52.311
We could have played it safe and
stripped away visual interest until

00:20:52.311 --> 00:20:55.491
accessibility became an easier
target, but that would have diminished

00:20:55.493 --> 00:20:57.065
the experience for everyone.

00:20:57.957 --> 00:21:01.223
If the museum was challenging
assumptions about what an accessible

00:21:01.231 --> 00:21:04.187
physical experience could be, we
wanted the website to challenge

00:21:04.187 --> 00:21:06.474
those same, uh, assumptions online.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:12.476
To put it simply, if you imagine
that a highly aesthetic and immersive

00:21:12.476 --> 00:21:16.572
website design is one circle and
accessibility is another, we were

00:21:16.573 --> 00:21:18.553
looking for the overlap between the two.

00:21:19.088 --> 00:21:22.485
And rather than just accepting that
this common ground was a small sliver

00:21:22.485 --> 00:21:27.067
to work within, we were looking at this
project as a way to expand that overlap.

00:21:28.676 --> 00:21:33.162
An example of this work on the website is
the decorative neumorphic Braille headers.

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:37.375
We drew inspiration directly
from the in-person experience.

00:21:38.146 --> 00:21:41.830
The exhibits provide a traditional
title and description with tactile

00:21:41.833 --> 00:21:46.137
Braille, giving visitors different ways
to encounter the same information based

00:21:46.138 --> 00:21:47.859
on how they experience the museum.

00:21:49.551 --> 00:21:53.527
We wanted to bring that idea
into the website by adding

00:21:53.531 --> 00:21:55.597
Braille to support our H1s.

00:21:56.295 --> 00:22:00.405
The neumorphic style gives the Braille
just a subtle sense of depth, visually

00:22:00.429 --> 00:22:06.072
echoing the tactile in-person experience
and reinforcing a simple truth: everyone

00:22:06.072 --> 00:22:07.520
encounters the world differently.

00:22:08.987 --> 00:22:11.749
But that also raised an
important accessibility question.

00:22:12.533 --> 00:22:16.158
Neumorphic design relies on low
contrast to create a sense of depth

00:22:16.426 --> 00:22:21.110
visually, so we had to ask ourselves,
does using this style create a

00:22:21.111 --> 00:22:22.748
barrier for some of our users?

00:22:23.802 --> 00:22:26.652
That question pushed us to think
carefully about the role of Braille

00:22:26.652 --> 00:22:30.489
in the design and whether it was
providing essential information or

00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:34.779
actor, or acting as a decorative
extension of the museum experience.

00:22:37.216 --> 00:22:40.773
We started each design decision
with a few primary questions.

00:22:41.005 --> 00:22:44.648
A ruber, a rubric we could
use to determine whether an

00:22:44.650 --> 00:22:46.406
experience was truly inclusive.

00:22:47.337 --> 00:22:49.256
What is the purpose of this element?

00:22:49.511 --> 00:22:52.079
What is each audience
able to take away here?

00:22:52.473 --> 00:22:57.176
And is one audience getting information
that another audience will not be able to?

00:22:58.458 --> 00:23:03.425
So with the Braille in question, let's put
that treatment through that same rubric.

00:23:04.145 --> 00:23:06.158
What is the purpose of this element?

00:23:08.105 --> 00:23:12.085
The Braille is intended to be educational
for our sighted community who may not

00:23:12.085 --> 00:23:16.606
have been exposed to Braille previously,
while also mimicking the inexper--

00:23:16.854 --> 00:23:19.099
the in-person experience for everyone.

00:23:20.492 --> 00:23:22.770
What is each audience
able to take away here?

00:23:23.430 --> 00:23:26.896
For sighted users, the Braille itself
becomes part of the experience,

00:23:27.167 --> 00:23:31.294
something they can see, engage with, and
potentially become more curious about.

00:23:32.427 --> 00:23:36.541
While someone using a screen reader,
seeing that Braille isn't necessary, what

00:23:36.541 --> 00:23:40.668
matters is the understanding of what the
Braille represents and why it's there.

00:23:42.284 --> 00:23:44.882
And lastly, is one audience
getting information that

00:23:44.882 --> 00:23:46.416
another will not be able to?

00:23:47.593 --> 00:23:52.267
Both audience, both audiences here
receive the same core message, but

00:23:52.271 --> 00:23:56.311
through experiences designed for how they
specifically interact with the content.

00:23:59.001 --> 00:24:01.774
The Braille was applied with
a few intentional guardrails.

00:24:02.445 --> 00:24:06.253
First, we pushed the contrast as far
as we could while still maintaining

00:24:06.264 --> 00:24:08.201
the neumorphic look and sense of depth.

00:24:09.030 --> 00:24:12.274
Second, the Braille only
mirrors an existing H1.

00:24:12.943 --> 00:24:17.482
It will never introduce new or additive
information, so nothing is lost if

00:24:17.482 --> 00:24:19.392
someone cannot perceive it visually.

00:24:20.691 --> 00:24:23.890
And third, we made sure assistive
technology could understand

00:24:23.952 --> 00:24:25.394
what was happening visually.

00:24:25.994 --> 00:24:28.698
We added an ARIA label
that provides that context.

00:24:29.289 --> 00:24:34.243
For the example of our exhibits and
experience page, it reads, "Animated

00:24:34.281 --> 00:24:38.559
uncontracted Braille rendering of the
page title Exhibits and Experiences."

00:24:40.772 --> 00:24:43.739
With these parameters in place,
we were able to answer our key

00:24:43.744 --> 00:24:46.598
accessibility questions and move
forward with the Braille as part

00:24:46.598 --> 00:24:48.225
of the site's visual language.

00:24:49.139 --> 00:24:53.305
It also gave us an opportunity to develop
something special for The Dot Experience,

00:24:53.486 --> 00:24:55.435
our very own text-to-Braille converter.

00:24:56.355 --> 00:24:59.762
Rather than manually creating each
treatment, we developed a system

00:24:59.762 --> 00:25:03.216
that could take the page title
and translate it into uncontracted

00:25:03.217 --> 00:25:06.914
Braille, allowing the effect to be
used consistently throughout the site.

00:25:11.226 --> 00:25:16.572
Carl: The brown headers are one visible
example of accessibility, but much of the

00:25:16.573 --> 00:25:21.503
important work happens behind the scenes
in how the website is actually built.

00:25:22.288 --> 00:25:23.793
Let's take a look at that next.

00:25:26.524 --> 00:25:31.550
Accessibility could not be something
that we add after the website was built.

00:25:31.940 --> 00:25:35.686
It had to be part of the
foundation from the very beginning.

00:25:36.312 --> 00:25:41.086
For us, that meant focusing on
three key pillars that shaped how

00:25:41.087 --> 00:25:42.750
we approached the entire build.

00:25:44.922 --> 00:25:47.583
It all starts with semantic HTML.

00:25:48.406 --> 00:25:50.717
A heading needs to read as a real heading.

00:25:51.337 --> 00:25:53.367
A button needs to be a real button.

00:25:53.941 --> 00:25:57.356
Navigation needs to be
identified as navigation.

00:25:58.236 --> 00:26:03.155
Forms need to have clear labels and
instructions and error messaging.

00:26:04.753 --> 00:26:09.088
Those structural choices give assistive
tools the information that they

00:26:09.090 --> 00:26:13.480
need to understand not just what's
on the page, but how each one of

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:15.013
those things relate to one another.

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:21.264
Sighted users may be able to understand
those relationships through size,

00:26:21.265 --> 00:26:23.773
spacing, color, and position.

00:26:24.586 --> 00:26:28.280
Screen reader users often depend
on the underlying structure

00:26:28.779 --> 00:26:32.418
to me-- to communicate the
same hierarchy and meaning.

00:26:33.399 --> 00:26:37.949
Good semantic HTML helps make
sure that that information is

00:26:37.967 --> 00:26:42.227
not simply lost because someone
experiences the page differently.

00:26:45.688 --> 00:26:48.410
We also focused closely on interaction.

00:26:49.180 --> 00:26:53.255
Every interactive element needed
to work without relying on a mouse.

00:26:53.940 --> 00:26:58.551
Users had to be able to navigate with
a keyboard, clearly understand where

00:26:58.552 --> 00:27:03.674
their focus was, operate controls,
and complete tasks without getting

00:27:03.675 --> 00:27:05.308
trapped or losing their place.

00:27:06.404 --> 00:27:10.655
But it wasn't enough for something
to technically respond to a keyboard.

00:27:11.312 --> 00:27:15.325
It also needed to behave in a way
that felt logical and predictable.

00:27:16.147 --> 00:27:19.874
When a menu or modal opens,
where does the focus go?

00:27:20.406 --> 00:27:23.176
When it closes, where
does that focus return?

00:27:23.989 --> 00:27:27.173
Can the user tell which
element is currently active?

00:27:27.798 --> 00:27:31.013
Does a control communicate
whether it is expanded,

00:27:31.520 --> 00:27:33.704
collapsed, selected, or disabled?

00:27:34.880 --> 00:27:39.783
Those details can feel small, but
together they determine whether an

00:27:39.966 --> 00:27:45.472
interface feels intuitive or frustrating
for someone navigating without a mouse.

00:27:48.730 --> 00:27:51.803
The third part of the
foundation was reusability.

00:27:52.659 --> 00:27:56.823
We were building a WordPress
system that the museum staff would

00:27:56.823 --> 00:27:59.154
continue using long after launch.

00:27:59.890 --> 00:28:03.175
We could not depend on someone
remembering a long accessibility

00:28:03.185 --> 00:28:05.734
checklist every time they created a page.

00:28:06.783 --> 00:28:12.524
So we built accessibility behaviors
into reusable components and established

00:28:12.536 --> 00:28:19.028
consistent patterns for headings,
buttons, links, navigation, form,

00:28:19.097 --> 00:28:21.993
media, and other content blocks.

00:28:22.927 --> 00:28:28.929
Content authors would still need to write
meaningful link text, provide appropriate

00:28:28.964 --> 00:28:34.625
image descriptions, and organize
content clearly, but the system should

00:28:34.650 --> 00:28:37.148
guide them towards accessible choices.

00:28:40.610 --> 00:28:44.081
Danielle: What is the user testing
look like for The DOT Experience?

00:28:44.572 --> 00:28:50.525
So in order to thoroughly test
this website, we had to ensure that

00:28:50.908 --> 00:28:56.663
we had a, a group of testers that
had a variety of disabilities and

00:28:56.663 --> 00:29:02.820
utilized a variety of assistive
technology devices and platforms.

00:29:03.285 --> 00:29:09.506
Some of these included using a
PC with JAWS screen reader or

00:29:09.567 --> 00:29:12.569
NVDA, Non-Visual Desktop Access.

00:29:13.451 --> 00:29:17.399
Some of our users also
used VoiceOver on a Mac.

00:29:17.752 --> 00:29:24.621
We also had users that utilized screen
magnification both on the PC and the Mac.

00:29:24.853 --> 00:29:31.536
So having this wide variety of
testers, we were able to catch a--

00:29:32.879 --> 00:29:39.635
more accessibility, uh, errors than we
would have caught just with one device,

00:29:39.668 --> 00:29:45.853
and this allowed more fine-tuning of
the website for maximum accessibility.

00:29:46.361 --> 00:29:51.945
We also had users who used refreshable
Braille displays connected to their

00:29:51.945 --> 00:29:58.293
computers, and that was also tested
to ensure that Braille display users

00:29:58.293 --> 00:30:02.962
could access the same material that
was spoken to the screen reader user.

00:30:04.062 --> 00:30:10.123
Having all of this together ensures
that not only is a website accessible

00:30:10.141 --> 00:30:14.759
for individuals that use screen
reading technology, but it's also

00:30:15.197 --> 00:30:20.330
accessible for individuals who use
other types of assistive technology,

00:30:20.341 --> 00:30:28.149
such as im- alternative input devices,
eye-tracking devices, and other types

00:30:28.149 --> 00:30:35.283
of technology that would require the
use of good keyboard navigation and the

00:30:35.283 --> 00:30:38.295
ability to navigate from item to item.

00:30:41.088 --> 00:30:44.468
Melody: This was an exciting but
also slightly nerve-wracking moment.

00:30:44.688 --> 00:30:49.192
We had spent a ton of time learning,
questioning our assumptions, and

00:30:49.204 --> 00:30:52.498
ultimately building this experience
that we felt was hitting the

00:30:52.499 --> 00:30:54.369
mar- uh, mark of inclusivity.

00:30:55.005 --> 00:30:59.391
But by leaning into this group of
individuals, we were going to find

00:30:59.393 --> 00:31:01.266
out how well we had actually done.

00:31:01.859 --> 00:31:07.899
Between the team that Danielle described
and then a consultant of accessibility

00:31:07.909 --> 00:31:12.896
experts, we were able to jump in and
find out what was working, what was

00:31:12.898 --> 00:31:15.061
causing issues, and how we could improve.

00:31:16.252 --> 00:31:19.256
As expected, even though we
did all of that upfront work,

00:31:19.312 --> 00:31:20.669
we still received notes.

00:31:21.422 --> 00:31:24.503
New things were added to our
accessibility knowledge base that

00:31:24.514 --> 00:31:26.365
shifted this project forward.

00:31:27.188 --> 00:31:32.312
This larger perspective that we could
not have internally allowed us to move to

00:31:32.312 --> 00:31:34.538
launch much more confident and excited.

00:31:37.688 --> 00:31:41.581
This testing reinforced one of the
biggest lessons from this project: there's

00:31:41.652 --> 00:31:43.735
always room for another perspective.

00:31:44.283 --> 00:31:48.240
We had done the research, followed
standards, and had built accessibility

00:31:48.250 --> 00:31:51.814
into the design and development process
from the very beginning, but these

00:31:51.826 --> 00:31:56.427
Braille users still s- taught us things
that we had not yet considered, and

00:31:56.427 --> 00:31:58.126
that is just part of the process.

00:31:58.720 --> 00:32:03.313
In that respect, can we ever really call
a website fully accessible and complete?

00:32:05.790 --> 00:32:09.617
Because of this, we make gathering
feedback part of our online experience.

00:32:10.109 --> 00:32:14.505
We created paths on the website for users
to tell us when something wasn't working

00:32:14.508 --> 00:32:19.552
for them or when they interacted with a
barrier that we just did not anticipate.

00:32:20.226 --> 00:32:23.831
Because launching an accessible website
does not mean your work is done.

00:32:24.636 --> 00:32:28.879
The ongoing feedback allows us to
continue to refine what an inclusive

00:32:28.879 --> 00:32:31.478
web experience should be moving forward.

00:32:33.883 --> 00:32:36.315
So what do we take from all of this?

00:32:37.313 --> 00:32:41.871
The Dot Experience changed the way
we approached accessibility, not just

00:32:41.872 --> 00:32:46.530
for the, this website, but for every
website Fieldtrip touches in the future.

00:32:47.087 --> 00:32:51.065
And to understand how, let's go back
to the assumptions we started with.

00:32:54.011 --> 00:32:58.832
Danielle: So to go back to the
question, do you have to choose

00:32:58.892 --> 00:33:03.230
between accessibility and design?

00:33:03.562 --> 00:33:04.893
The answer is no.

00:33:05.158 --> 00:33:12.375
You can create an accessible website that
is fully navigable by a wide range of

00:33:12.416 --> 00:33:20.485
users with various disabilities, but also
create a visually appealing website that

00:33:20.687 --> 00:33:24.501
looks great and works well for everyone

00:33:26.718 --> 00:33:31.546
Melody: The most impactful experiences
are both accessible and visually engaging.

00:33:31.706 --> 00:33:36.570
It is Fieldtrip's belief now that
accessibility should not be viewed as a

00:33:36.570 --> 00:33:41.337
creative constraint, rather an opportunity
to design better for everyone who's going

00:33:41.337 --> 00:33:43.000
to be interacting with your website.

00:33:45.614 --> 00:33:50.589
Carl: So the assumption of a
accessible plugin is enough.

00:33:51.249 --> 00:33:52.133
Here's what we learned.

00:33:53.012 --> 00:33:55.066
Accessibility is built.

00:33:55.253 --> 00:33:56.302
It's not installed.

00:33:57.174 --> 00:34:03.476
A plugin can help identify or address
certain issues, but it cannot replace

00:34:03.616 --> 00:34:05.921
intentional design and development.

00:34:06.934 --> 00:34:12.937
True accessibility begins with semantic
structure, keyboard-friendly interactions,

00:34:13.520 --> 00:34:17.684
clear content, and components that
are built correctly from the start.

00:34:18.690 --> 00:34:24.087
Plugins can be useful tools, but
they are a supplement to accessible

00:34:24.087 --> 00:34:26.399
development, not a substitute for it.

00:34:30.330 --> 00:34:34.130
Melody: And finally, we assume that
doing accessibility right would require

00:34:34.130 --> 00:34:39.860
a much more custom approach, one that
ultimately would add time and cost to

00:34:39.861 --> 00:34:41.763
the website projects of our clients.

00:34:42.927 --> 00:34:46.369
What we learned through this project
is that accessibility does not have

00:34:46.370 --> 00:34:47.852
to be a separate phase of work.

00:34:48.663 --> 00:34:52.125
When it's considered from the very
beginning, it becomes part of how

00:34:52.126 --> 00:34:54.381
you strategize, design, and develop.

00:34:55.543 --> 00:34:58.179
You choose the right semantic
structure the first time.

00:34:58.467 --> 00:35:02.185
You build keyboard interactions into
the component from the very start.

00:35:02.719 --> 00:35:06.438
You establish accessible patterns that
can be reused throughout the site.

00:35:07.541 --> 00:35:11.657
There is still time and expertise
involved, of course, but the bigger

00:35:11.658 --> 00:35:15.177
lift comes when accessibility is
treated as something you go back and

00:35:15.191 --> 00:35:19.391
add later when you're re-engineering
your site to hit these guidelines.

00:35:20.707 --> 00:35:24.162
Once you learn how to build with
accessibility in mind, it simply

00:35:24.163 --> 00:35:27.529
becomes part of how you build
these websites moving forward.

00:35:30.622 --> 00:35:33.348
Now, if you're watching this
presentation today, there's a

00:35:33.349 --> 00:35:37.175
good chance that you share in the
responsibility of making the web a

00:35:37.176 --> 00:35:39.355
more inclusive space in some capacity.

00:35:39.943 --> 00:35:45.659
Whether you're a designer, developer,
project manager, copywriter, or client,

00:35:45.974 --> 00:35:50.671
you have the opportunity to challenge
assumptions, ask better questions, and

00:35:50.673 --> 00:35:53.165
invite more voices into your processes.

00:35:54.238 --> 00:35:58.143
For more than 165 years, APH has
worked to break down barriers for

00:35:58.150 --> 00:36:00.181
people who are blind and low vision.

00:36:00.746 --> 00:36:04.330
The Dot Experience challenged us to think
about what it looks like to carry that

00:36:04.331 --> 00:36:06.616
same commitment into the digital world.

00:36:08.140 --> 00:36:11.845
And what we learned is that accessibility
isn't about getting every box

00:36:11.846 --> 00:36:14.863
checked or reaching a point where
you can say the work is complete.

00:36:15.624 --> 00:36:19.551
It's about creating the foundation
that allows more people to f- fully

00:36:19.575 --> 00:36:21.251
participate in the experience.

00:36:22.432 --> 00:36:26.394
So if you walk away with one thing from
our time together today, let it be this:

00:36:26.873 --> 00:36:29.067
accessibility isn't the finish line.

00:36:29.151 --> 00:36:30.678
It's where inclusion begins.

00:36:41.188 --> 00:36:45.077
Sherose: Oh, thank you so much for joining
us, uh, Danielle, Carl, and Melody.

00:36:45.411 --> 00:36:51.514
We have a ton of questions in the Q&A and
about 10 or so minutes to address them.

00:36:51.828 --> 00:36:53.948
Let me start with a quick one first.

00:36:54.252 --> 00:36:57.928
Could you describe what is meant
by uncontracted, um, Braille?

00:37:00.069 --> 00:37:01.906
Danielle: Uh, this is
Danielle, I can take that.

00:37:02.498 --> 00:37:08.015
Um, uncontracted Braille is every
letter is spelled out versus contracted

00:37:08.022 --> 00:37:12.397
Braille, which is the standard Braille
code, meaning that there are symbols

00:37:12.403 --> 00:37:14.531
to represent groups of letters.

00:37:14.895 --> 00:37:19.058
And most, uh, Braille readers
read standard contracted Braille,

00:37:19.336 --> 00:37:23.453
and we generally start beginning
Braille users with uncontracted.

00:37:25.016 --> 00:37:25.870
Sherose: Thank you.

00:37:25.971 --> 00:37:31.194
Um, and then we have some, uh,
some questions about, uh, reusable

00:37:31.209 --> 00:37:32.384
content blocks in WordPress.

00:37:32.391 --> 00:37:36.395
So one question asks what WordPress
design system did you use, like Block

00:37:36.395 --> 00:37:40.076
Themes or Theme Builder, and another
person wanted to know, um, if there

00:37:40.076 --> 00:37:44.118
was, like, a design system or framework
that made creating these foundations,

00:37:44.162 --> 00:37:45.736
foundations easier for your team.

00:37:46.632 --> 00:37:52.308
Carl: Um, so we do not use
any, um, third-party themes or

00:37:52.341 --> 00:37:54.106
anything like that at Fieldtrip.

00:37:54.106 --> 00:37:55.480
We kind of start from the ground up.

00:37:55.506 --> 00:37:58.411
Now, we do have kind of like a
starter theme that we've built for

00:37:58.412 --> 00:38:03.772
ourselves, um, but we don't use any
preexisting design systems or any

00:38:03.779 --> 00:38:05.901
CSS frameworks or anything like that.

00:38:06.547 --> 00:38:11.495
Um, in terms of how we built out
those res- reusable components,

00:38:11.544 --> 00:38:17.199
um, it was not a, um, a theme
builder or anything like that.

00:38:17.230 --> 00:38:21.203
It was just going back to, uh,
advanced custom fields, their

00:38:21.205 --> 00:38:26.153
flexible content approach, and
building out, um, essentially those

00:38:26.165 --> 00:38:27.935
content blocks that we referred to.

00:38:29.089 --> 00:38:31.341
Sherose: Were there any themes
that you may have, like, checked

00:38:31.341 --> 00:38:32.978
before deciding to go that route?

00:38:33.986 --> 00:38:34.386
Carl: No.

00:38:34.388 --> 00:38:36.902
We, we really do not
use themes at Fieldtrip.

00:38:36.926 --> 00:38:39.580
We start kind of everything
from the ground up here.

00:38:40.022 --> 00:38:40.629
Sherose: Interesting.

00:38:40.636 --> 00:38:41.069
Interesting.

00:38:41.077 --> 00:38:41.520
Thank you.

00:38:41.933 --> 00:38:44.341
Um, the next question for Danielle.

00:38:44.404 --> 00:38:48.477
Um, do you think that testing with one
screen reader is good enough, or is

00:38:48.478 --> 00:38:50.285
it important to test with multiple?

00:38:50.736 --> 00:38:54.712
Um, how important is testing on
mobile and with mobile screen

00:38:54.714 --> 00:38:56.480
readers on a website like this?

00:38:57.480 --> 00:39:03.495
Danielle: So I would say you would
need to use multiple screen readers.

00:39:03.741 --> 00:39:09.134
One reason is, um, you know, there's
a wide variety of personal preferences

00:39:09.134 --> 00:39:10.616
in screen readers these days.

00:39:10.862 --> 00:39:14.035
Not everybody uses JAWS, for example.

00:39:14.069 --> 00:39:16.490
NVDA has become very popular.

00:39:16.852 --> 00:39:21.875
So I think, you know, especially for,
you know, this day and time, you need

00:39:22.091 --> 00:39:24.187
to test with multiple screen readers.

00:39:24.221 --> 00:39:29.520
And I also think that it's actually
even more important to test on mobile.

00:39:30.053 --> 00:39:35.477
Most individuals who are blind or
low vision is more likely to access

00:39:35.589 --> 00:39:42.452
digital content on their phone than
they are the computer in this, you

00:39:42.452 --> 00:39:44.571
know, current stream of technology, so.

00:39:45.781 --> 00:39:49.554
Sherose: That's really interesting,
um, that mobile should pr- It sounds

00:39:49.554 --> 00:39:54.075
like mobile, um, experience should
be prioritized when, um, developing,

00:39:54.327 --> 00:39:55.752
uh, with accessibility in mind.

00:39:55.854 --> 00:39:57.381
Is, am I understanding you correctly?

00:39:58.058 --> 00:39:58.613
Danielle: Yes.

00:39:58.670 --> 00:40:02.866
A- and honestly, es- especially for
older adults who are blind or low vision,

00:40:02.875 --> 00:40:06.788
many of them do not even… Some of
them… I have friends who don't even

00:40:06.790 --> 00:40:13.164
have a computer that's working right now
And so I think it's h- very important

00:40:13.520 --> 00:40:15.968
to take that into consideration.

00:40:16.180 --> 00:40:21.822
Not only that, mobile is generally easier,
especially for a deaf/blind person who's

00:40:21.822 --> 00:40:24.252
using a Braille display to navigate.

00:40:24.283 --> 00:40:29.990
I personally find it easier to
navigate on mobile than a cluttered

00:40:30.004 --> 00:40:31.699
desktop version of a website.

00:40:32.693 --> 00:40:33.378
Sherose: Interesting.

00:40:33.579 --> 00:40:38.330
Would you mind maybe expanding,
like, um, what are some common

00:40:38.330 --> 00:40:42.665
accessibility barriers you experience
across mobile sites that WordPress

00:40:42.667 --> 00:40:44.080
developers should be aware of?

00:40:45.130 --> 00:40:45.573
Danielle: Sure.

00:40:45.623 --> 00:40:48.570
Um, I think one of the
big ones is a lot of ads.

00:40:48.602 --> 00:40:53.361
I, um, like to do… I, I, I
like to cook, so I'm on a lot

00:40:53.362 --> 00:40:55.401
of recipe websites, for example.

00:40:56.077 --> 00:40:57.001
Probably too much.

00:40:57.253 --> 00:41:04.734
And one of the things that I encounter a
lot is I get stuck in these ads, and then

00:41:04.736 --> 00:41:09.330
I'm trying to get out of it, and all I
really want is just to read the recipe.

00:41:09.330 --> 00:41:15.699
And, and there's been occasions where I've
tested the link to, you know, my sighted

00:41:15.822 --> 00:41:17.983
husband and said, "Here, type it out.

00:41:18.278 --> 00:41:22.710
I'm done fighting it." So I
think for me that's the big one.

00:41:22.710 --> 00:41:27.158
And ha- you know, and also having good
heading navigation is very helpful.

00:41:27.180 --> 00:41:28.326
Or even a link.

00:41:28.364 --> 00:41:32.299
I, I love when the link says,
"Jump to recipes." So those

00:41:32.299 --> 00:41:34.130
are things to keep in mind.

00:41:34.366 --> 00:41:34.738
Sherose: Yeah.

00:41:34.738 --> 00:41:37.408
What I'm hearing from you is, is the
semantic structure that y'all were

00:41:37.408 --> 00:41:40.824
speaking of earlier is what really
matters here, and having a clean and

00:41:40.827 --> 00:41:43.889
easy-to-understand navigation like
you do on The Dot Museum website.

00:41:44.115 --> 00:41:47.160
Like, these are all things that,
that translate well in mobile

00:41:47.206 --> 00:41:48.623
and matter to your experience.

00:41:49.221 --> 00:41:49.374
Danielle: Yes.

00:41:49.435 --> 00:41:49.688
Uh,

00:41:49.706 --> 00:41:53.776
Carl: you know, I, I'd like to add that-
Mm-hmm … in this experience for myself

00:41:53.777 --> 00:41:59.224
as a developer for many years and l-
learning what it takes to build something

00:41:59.226 --> 00:42:06.455
like this, is, um, I think accessibility
can be over-complicated because we

00:42:06.455 --> 00:42:10.342
have a lot of things that are already
pre-made for us that people have, uh,

00:42:10.342 --> 00:42:15.062
you know, foundations and, uh, browsers
and things like that from years past.

00:42:15.064 --> 00:42:20.481
I, I saw a comment, uh, that someone
made about 2005 semantic HTML, and

00:42:20.481 --> 00:42:25.972
now we're saying it again in 2026,
um, in that there's things that are

00:42:25.973 --> 00:42:31.487
already at our disposable to make
these things easier, um, and we should

00:42:31.487 --> 00:42:34.486
just use them as opposed to getting
in our own way, if that makes sense.

00:42:34.798 --> 00:42:35.324
Sherose: Yeah.

00:42:35.422 --> 00:42:36.165
Yeah, uh, course.

00:42:36.178 --> 00:42:37.014
Yeah, absolutely.

00:42:37.490 --> 00:42:39.164
Um, I have another technical question.

00:42:39.176 --> 00:42:43.610
What are some non-screen reader tools
that you used during the discovery phase?

00:42:45.728 --> 00:42:50.052
Carl: Um, so we had some br- uh,
Chrome browser extensions like ARC

00:42:50.125 --> 00:42:54.804
and Axe that helped us just identify,
like looking at websites that we

00:42:54.804 --> 00:42:57.530
had built in the past to see, uh- Mm

00:42:57.530 --> 00:42:59.611
what failed, what didn't work.

00:43:00.007 --> 00:43:03.878
And that was more kind of looking
at the HTML and things like that.

00:43:04.364 --> 00:43:09.312
But really, other than JAWS, we
were using, uh, VoiceOver for

00:43:09.369 --> 00:43:11.974
the, uh, Apple, Apple ecosystem.

00:43:13.071 --> 00:43:13.342
Sherose: Okay.

00:43:13.345 --> 00:43:13.777
Wonderful.

00:43:13.784 --> 00:43:14.258
Thank you.

00:43:14.626 --> 00:43:18.557
Um, the next question, uh,
probably short answer here.

00:43:18.563 --> 00:43:21.116
How long did it take, um,
to complete this project?

00:43:23.365 --> 00:43:24.808
Melody: That's a great question.

00:43:24.808 --> 00:43:27.529
We, uh, worked it in two different phases.

00:43:27.533 --> 00:43:30.709
So we actually started with
just the learning and discovery.

00:43:31.094 --> 00:43:35.193
And if I'm remembering correctly, that
was probably around, uh, four months.

00:43:35.395 --> 00:43:40.175
And then we moved into the actual,
uh, design and development, and that

00:43:40.175 --> 00:43:42.728
was probably another five or six.

00:43:43.301 --> 00:43:46.432
Can't remember exacts at this stage,
but we d- we did take our time

00:43:46.433 --> 00:43:50.764
and do many rounds of revisions
and review, but from, um, all the

00:43:50.764 --> 00:43:52.992
way back to the design portion.

00:43:53.065 --> 00:43:56.892
Um, and then obviously when we had
an actual developed experience, we

00:43:56.892 --> 00:43:58.662
could send through further testing.

00:43:59.446 --> 00:44:04.232
Sherose: Y- you mentioned design, um, and
I wanted to ask, is there, um, a design

00:44:04.260 --> 00:44:09.018
element that became, uh, a barrier, like
perhaps an insurmountable barrier when

00:44:09.019 --> 00:44:11.030
trying to make the website accessible?

00:44:11.772 --> 00:44:12.896
And how did you get around that?

00:44:16.227 --> 00:44:19.303
Carl: Um, this isn't necessarily
from a design perspective, and

00:44:19.308 --> 00:44:22.529
someone actually mentioned it in the
comments earlier, but one of the big

00:44:22.531 --> 00:44:26.691
surprises for me, uh, was video embeds.

00:44:26.808 --> 00:44:33.336
Um, and that things like YouTube
and Vimeo, those players are

00:44:33.337 --> 00:44:35.087
actually not very accessible at all.

00:44:35.754 --> 00:44:38.819
Um, and so that was a
big challenge for us.

00:44:38.872 --> 00:44:42.783
And if I'm being completely honest,
I think we still have some room

00:44:42.830 --> 00:44:46.511
to improve there, um, in terms
of how we've implemented that.

00:44:46.511 --> 00:44:52.977
We tried our best with, um, uh, putting
it inside of accessible players.

00:44:53.459 --> 00:44:55.575
Uh, so in this case it was Able Player.

00:44:56.136 --> 00:44:59.544
Um, but that was a big surprise for me.

00:44:59.734 --> 00:45:04.859
With such large company, w- you know,
those two large companies, um, and

00:45:04.859 --> 00:45:10.566
learning that, you know, those embeds
are all over the internet and so, um,

00:45:10.578 --> 00:45:12.979
that those things are not the greatest.

00:45:13.944 --> 00:45:14.462
Sherose: They're not.

00:45:14.613 --> 00:45:15.009
They're not.

00:45:15.015 --> 00:45:20.176
And when they're hosting third-party, uh,
video players, it's so much harder to c-

00:45:20.221 --> 00:45:22.169
uh, control the experience for your user.

00:45:22.216 --> 00:45:22.654
Excellent.

00:45:22.671 --> 00:45:22.734
Yeah.

00:45:23.026 --> 00:45:25.453
Um, okay, uh, we only have,
like, a minute and a half.

00:45:25.453 --> 00:45:26.884
I'll ask you a question.

00:45:27.124 --> 00:45:29.611
Um, you answered a little bit
in your presentation already.

00:45:29.837 --> 00:45:32.678
How do you approach ensuring that
content editors in the future

00:45:32.727 --> 00:45:34.486
continue to create accessible content?

00:45:34.511 --> 00:45:37.138
You did mention creating reusable
blocks, but was there any other

00:45:37.138 --> 00:45:38.185
support that you provided?

00:45:39.131 --> 00:45:42.982
Carl: Um, so one of the things that we
did, and it, you know, takes extra time

00:45:42.984 --> 00:45:48.102
to do this but it's worth it, is that
we leave notes and comments, uh, for the

00:45:48.102 --> 00:45:52.857
client as they're editing content to say,
"Hey, remember this, don't forget this.

00:45:52.872 --> 00:45:57.571
Best practice for that." Um, so that
was a sense of, like, a documentation

00:45:57.571 --> 00:46:01.627
of sort that kind of follows them along
as they're reusing those components.

00:46:02.544 --> 00:46:05.727
Melody: And when we were doing our
training on the website because our…

00:46:05.794 --> 00:46:10.709
You know, it is fully managed by our
client, uh, we also tried to remind and

00:46:10.723 --> 00:46:14.837
educate on some of these accessibility
principles because there are elements that

00:46:14.850 --> 00:46:19.569
go beyond our reach, the actual words on
the page and, uh, things of that nature

00:46:19.569 --> 00:46:23.803
that can quickly move an accessible site
into not a super accessible experience.

00:46:23.816 --> 00:46:27.049
So we try to pair the
education aspect as well.

00:46:27.772 --> 00:46:31.180
Sherose: Uh, Melody, w- do you
mean, like, stuff like, um, using

00:46:31.182 --> 00:46:35.062
plain language or, um, how to
write a heading, things like that?

00:46:35.305 --> 00:46:36.024
Melody: Yep, both.

00:46:36.069 --> 00:46:40.102
So the technical side of making sure
your heading structure is labeled and

00:46:40.102 --> 00:46:42.720
used properly in the back end, but
also all the way down to, like, what

00:46:42.720 --> 00:46:44.470
are the actual words on the page.

00:46:45.354 --> 00:46:45.750
Sherose: Thank you.

00:46:45.752 --> 00:46:46.252
Thank you.

00:46:46.511 --> 00:46:49.086
Um, I wanna thank you all for
attending this session with

00:46:49.095 --> 00:46:50.537
Carl, Melody and Danielle.

00:46:50.964 --> 00:46:53.901
Um, you can continue the conversation
in the chat or on social media

00:46:53.906 --> 00:47:00.551
using #WPAllyDay, um, and #WPAD2026.

